WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.545
Rust gives teams strong tools for memory
safety, ownership,

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:05.237
and explicit failure handling,

3
00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:09.497
but compiling successfully is not the same
as being robust, secure, maintainable,

4
00:00:09.577 --> 00:00:10.831
or operationally ready.

5
00:00:10.911 --> 00:00:14.613
A program can satisfy the compiler while
still hiding unclear ownership,

6
00:00:14.693 --> 00:00:17.918
fragile error paths, weak validation,
excessive resource use,

7
00:00:17.998 --> 00:00:20.505
or difficult-to-review lifecycle
assumptions.

8
00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:24.706
A Rust anti-pattern is a repeated habit
that works in the moment but creates future

9
00:00:24.786 --> 00:00:25.084
cost.

10
00:00:25.164 --> 00:00:28.090
It may start as a quick clone, a broad
lifetime annotation,

11
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.036
an `unwrap` in code that later becomes
production logic,

12
00:00:31.116 --> 00:00:33.504
a raw primitive passed through too many
layers,

13
00:00:33.584 --> 00:00:36.869
or an unsafe boundary that depends on
undocumented assumptions.

14
00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:40.054
None of these choices automatically means
the code is broken,

15
00:00:40.134 --> 00:00:41.865
but each can become a design smell.

16
00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:43.557
The useful posture is not blame.

17
00:00:43.637 --> 00:00:45.070
Anti-patterns are signals.

18
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.375
They show places where the code is asking
for clearer ownership,

19
00:00:48.455 --> 00:00:52.635
more deliberate error handling, stronger
types, safer boundaries, better tests,

20
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:54.745
or more explicit operational behavior.

21
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:58.945
Treating these signals early is cheaper
than discovering them after an incident,

22
00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.518
outage, or painful refactor.

23
00:01:00.598 --> 00:01:04.837
Rust's strengths are real, but they do not
remove the need for engineering judgment.

24
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:07.305
The compiler can enforce many borrowing
rules,

25
00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:10.729
but it cannot decide whether an API
exposes the right domain model,

26
00:01:10.809 --> 00:01:13.138
whether a dependency feature is
appropriate,

27
00:01:13.218 --> 00:01:15.248
whether a log line leaks sensitive data,

28
00:01:15.328 --> 00:01:17.896
or whether an async service has sane
backpressure.

29
00:01:17.976 --> 00:01:22.036
The defensive goal of this course is to
make weak patterns easier to recognize and

30
00:01:22.116 --> 00:01:24.027
safer alternatives easier to choose.

31
00:01:24.107 --> 00:01:27.988
Good Rust code makes ownership, errors,
data meaning, boundary validation,

32
00:01:28.068 --> 00:01:32.427
resource limits, and operational
assumptions visible enough for another engineer to

33
00:01:32.507 --> 00:01:33.940
review, test, and maintain.
